ire=0.95|Ladder line=0.91|Twin lead=0.82|Foam coax=0.8|RG-58 coax=0.66␀␀␀␀L = K*c/(4*f)␀␀␀L = K*c/(2*f)␀␀␀Total length␀␀␀␀Tip to tip. About 10 m for 14 MHz.␀␀How much shorter than a free-space half wave. 0.95 for thin wire.␀␀␀lambda = VF*c/f␀Wavelength␀␀One full cycle in space. The 2 m band is 2 m.␀␀␀FM radio is 100 MHz, Wi-Fi 2.4 GHz.␀VF␀␀Velocity factor␀Speed along the line as a share of c. 1 in free space.␀␀Free space=1|Bare wire=0.95|Ladder line=0.91|Twin lead=0.82|Foam coax=0.8|RG-58 coax=0.66␀␀␀fc = 1/(2*pi*R*C)␀␀␀fc␀␀f_{c}␀␀␀Cutoff frequency␀␀␀␀The -3 dB point.␀␀␀␀Resistance␀␀Of the filter.␀␀kohm␀␀␀␀nF␀␀Vout = Vin*(1 + Rf/Rg)␀␀Limited by the supply rails.␀␀␀␀On the + input.␀Rf␀␀R_{f}␀␀␀Feedback resistor␀␀␀Output to the inverting input.␀␀Rg␀␀R_{g}␀␀␀Ground resistor␀Inverting input to ground.␀␀Vout = -Vin*Rf/Rin␀␀Inverted, and limited by the supply rails.␀␀Into Rin.␀␀␀Output back to the inverting input.␀Rin␀R_{in}␀␀Input resistor␀␀Sets the input impedance too.␀␀␀t = 1.1*R*C␀Pulse width␀How long the output stays high.␀Timing resistor␀Between the supply and pins 6 and 7.␀␀␀␀Timing capacitor␀␀␀␀Pin 6 to ground.␀␀␀␀f = 1.44/((R1 + 2*R2)*C)␀␀␀␀D = (R1 + R2)/(R1 + 2*R2)␀␀␀R2 = (1 - D)*1.44/(f*C)␀R1 = (2*D - 1)*1.44/(f*C)␀␀␀R1 = R2*(2*D - 1)/(1 - D)␀␀␀Output of the astable.␀␀High time as a share of the period. Always over 50% here.␀␀␀R1␀␀R_{1}␀␀␀Resistor R1␀Pin 7 to the supply. Keep it above 1 kohm.␀␀R2␀␀R_{2}␀␀␀Resistor R2␀Pin 7 to pins 2 and 6.␀␀Pins 2 and 6 to ground.␀L = 10*log10(P/P0)␀␀0 dBm is 1 mW. A Wi-Fi radio sends about +20 dBm.␀␀␀The actual power.␀␀␀mW␀␀P0␀␀P_{0}␀␀␀Reference power␀1 mW for dBm; change to 1 W for dBW.␀␀␀␀G = 20*log10(V2/V1)␀Gain␀␀␀␀+6 dB is about double voltage, +20 dB ten times.␀␀␀␀Voltage out␀The voltage being compared.␀Voltage in␀␀The reference voltage, into the same impedance.␀G = 10*log10(P2/P1)␀+3 dB is about double power, +10 dB ten times, -3 dB half.␀␀P2␀␀P_{2}␀␀␀Power out␀␀␀The power being compared.␀␀␀P1␀␀P_{1}␀␀␀Power in␀␀␀␀The reference power.␀␀␀␀Q = f/BW␀␀␀␀Q factor␀␀␀␀Sharpness of a resonance.␀␀␀f_{0}␀␀␀Centre frequency␀␀␀␀The peak of the resonance.␀␀BW␀␀Between the two -3 dB points.␀␀␀Q = sqrt(L/C)/R␀Sharpness of a resonance. 10 to 100 is typical.␀Series resistance␀␀␀The losses. Smaller gives a higher Q.␀␀␀Of the tuned circuit.␀␀␀f = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(L*C))␀␀Resonant frequency␀␀Where X_L equals X_C.␀␀␀The coil of the tuned circuit.␀␀The capacitor of the tuned circuit.␀Z = sqrt(R^2 + (2*pi*f*L)^2)␀␀␀␀Impedance␀␀␀Of the series pair. Above R.␀␀␀␀In series.␀␀Z = sqrt(R^2 + 1/(2*pi*f*C)^2)␀␀Z = sqrt(R^2 + (Xl - Xc)^2)␀Magnitude of the total AC opposition.␀␀␀The resistive part.␀Xl␀␀X_{L}␀␀␀From the coil: 2Ï€fL.␀␀␀Xc␀␀X_{C}␀␀␀From the capacitor: 1/(2Ï€fC).␀␀Xl = 2*pi*f*L␀␀␀AC opposition, in ohms. Rises with frequency.␀␀␀Xc = 1/(2*pi*f*C)␀␀␀AC opposition, in ohms. Falls as frequency rises.␀␀␀Of the signal. 60 Hz mains, 1 kHz audio.␀␀␀␀The capacitor.␀␀I = V/R*(1 - exp(-R*t/L))␀␀␀At time t, starting from zero. Below V/R.␀␀␀Step applied to the coil.␀␀␀In series, including the coil's own.␀␀␀␀Since the switch closed.␀␀␀␀V = V0*exp(-t/(R*C))␀␀␀␀Capacitor voltage␀␀␀At time t. Below V0.␀␀␀␀V0␀␀V_{0}␀␀␀Starting voltage␀␀␀␀When the discharge began.␀␀␀Since the discharge began.␀␀What it discharges through.␀V = Vs*(1 - exp(-t/(R*C)))␀␀At time t, starting from zero. Below Vs.␀␀␀␀Vs␀␀V_{s}␀␀␀What it is charging towards.␀␀␀␀In series with the capacitor.␀␀␀The capacitor being charged.␀␀␀␀tau = L/R␀␀␀tau␀Ï„␀␀Time constant␀␀␀After one Ï„ the current has moved 63% of the way.␀␀A power choke might be 100 uH.␀␀tau = R*C␀␀␀After one Ï„ a capacitor has moved 63% of the way.␀␀100 nF, 10 uF...␀␀␀␀ohm␀Ohm's law and power␀ohms watts volts amps voltage current resistance power vir pvi joule heating dissipation␀␀␀␀Voltage, current, resistance and power in a resistive part. Any two give the other two.␀r-series␀␀␀␀Resistors in series␀add sum chain total two␀End to end, resistances add. The same current flows through both.␀␀␀r-parallel␀␀Resistors in parallel␀␀␀product over sum shunt total two␀␀␀␀Side by side, the total is less than either one. Both see the same voltage.␀r-series3␀␀␀Three resistors in series␀␀␀add sum chain total␀End to end, resistances add.␀␀␀␀r-parallel3␀Three resistors in parallel␀shunt total␀Side by side, conductances add, so the reciprocals of the resistances do.␀␀␀Voltage divider␀potential divider attenuator sensor bias␀␀␀␀Two resistors tapping a fraction of a voltage. Assumes nothing heavy loads the output.␀␀cdivider␀␀␀␀Current divider␀split parallel branch share shunt␀␀␀How a current splits between two parallel resistors.␀␀␀␀LED resistor␀␀␀␀diode series limiting current light indicator␀␀␀The series resistor that sets an LED's current, and the heat it has to shed.␀␀␀␀c-parallel␀␀Capacitors in parallel␀␀add sum total␀␀␀Side by side, capacitances add.␀c-series␀␀␀␀Capacitors in series␀␀␀␀total product over sum␀␀End to end, the total is less than either one.␀␀l-series␀␀␀␀Inductors in series␀add sum total coil␀␀End to end, inductances add, as long as their fields do not couple.␀l-parallel␀␀Inductors in parallel␀␀␀Side by side, the total is less than either one, as long as their fields do not couple.␀wire-r␀␀Wire resistance␀resistivity gauge awg copper cable conductor␀␀␀␀Resistance from the metal, its length and its thickness.␀␀␀␀vdrop␀␀␀Cable voltage drop␀␀wire loss run length gauge sizing␀␀␀Volts lost in a two-conductor cable, out and back.␀␀r-temp␀␀Resistance change with temperature␀␀tempco coefficient alpha hot␀␀␀␀Metals resist more as they warm. Linear, so good for a hundred kelvin or so.␀␀␀␀battery-life␀␀␀␀Battery life␀␀␀␀runtime capacity mah ah hours discharge␀Run time from capacity and load. Real cells give less at high current.␀␀battery-energy␀␀Battery energy␀␀wh watt hours stored capacity␀␀␀Energy stored in a battery from its voltage and capacity.␀␀␀energy-use␀␀Electrical energy␀␀␀kwh bill consumption power time␀Energy used at a steady power. 1 kWh is 3.6 MJ.␀transformer␀Transformer voltage␀turns ratio step down up primary secondary winding␀␀Voltage goes with turns, for an ideal transformer.␀␀transformer-i␀␀␀Transformer current␀turns ratio amps primary secondary winding␀␀Current goes against turns: power in equals power out.␀␀rms␀RMS and peak␀␀␀␀sine ac average peak to peak pp mains waveform␀␀The readings of a sine wave. These ratios are for a sine only.␀␀ac-power␀␀␀␀AC power␀␀␀␀single phase watts real pf␀␀Real power in a single-phase AC load.␀␀␀three-phase␀Three-phase power␀␀␀3 phase motor kw watts line root 3␀␀Real power in a balanced three-phase load, from line voltage and line current.␀␀power-triangle␀␀Power factor and the power triangle␀pf real reactive apparent var va phase angle correction␀How real, reactive and apparent power fit a right triangle.␀efficiency␀␀Efficiency␀␀losses power in out eta␀The share of the power going in that comes out as something useful.␀internal-r␀␀Internal resistance␀battery sag esr source terminal load␀␀␀␀Why a source's voltage sags as it delivers current.␀Vt = E - I*r␀␀␀␀Vt␀␀V_{t}␀␀␀Terminal voltage␀␀␀␀What you measure under load.␀␀␀␀Open-circuit voltage␀␀␀␀With nothing connected. 1.5 V for a fresh AA.␀␀␀Drawn from the source.␀␀A AA about 0.2 ohm, a car battery 0.01 ohm.␀eta = Pout/Pin␀␀Î·␀␀Out over in. A good motor is 90%, a buck converter 95%.␀Pout␀␀␀␀P_{out}␀Useful power delivered.␀P_{in}␀␀Power taken from the source.␀␀␀␀S = sqrt(P^2 + Q^2)␀P = S*pf␀␀␀␀pf = cos(phi)␀␀␀Q = S*sin(phi)␀␀tan(phi) = Q/P␀␀Real power␀␀Does the work, in watts.␀␀␀␀Reactive power␀␀Sloshes back and forth; in VAR, shown here in W.␀␀␀␀Apparent power␀␀V times I; in VA, shown here in W.␀␀Power factor␀␀␀␀P over S. 1 is ideal.␀␀␀phi␀Ï†␀␀Phase angle␀Between voltage and current.␀␀␀␀P = sqrt(3)*V*I*pf␀␀Total of the three phases.␀␀kW␀␀V_{L}␀␀␀Line voltage␀␀␀␀Between two lines: 208, 400 or 480 V.␀␀␀Line current␀␀␀␀In each line.␀␀␀1 for heaters, about 0.85 for loaded motors.␀␀␀␀P = V*I*pf␀␀Doing work, in watts.␀␀␀RMS voltage␀US mains 120 V, Europe 230 V.␀␀␀RMS current␀As read by a clamp meter.␀␀␀1 for a heater, about 0.8 for a motor.␀␀Vrms = Vpk/sqrt(2)␀␀Vpp = 2*Vpk␀Vavg = 2*Vpk/pi␀Vrms␀␀␀␀V_{rms}␀The heating value and what a meter shows. US mains 120 V.␀␀␀V_{pk}␀␀Peak voltage␀␀␀␀From zero to the top of the wave. 170 V for 120 V mains.␀␀␀␀Vpp␀V_{pp}␀␀Peak-to-peak␀␀␀␀Bottom to top, as read off a scope.␀Vavg␀␀␀␀V_{avg}␀Rectified average␀␀␀Mean of the wave with its negative half flipped.␀␀␀␀Is/Ip = Np/Ns␀␀␀Ip␀␀I_{p}␀␀␀Primary current␀Drawn from the supply.␀␀Is␀␀I_{s}␀␀␀Secondary current␀␀␀Delivered to the load.␀␀Np␀␀N_{p}␀␀␀Primary turns␀␀␀Turns on the input winding.␀Ns␀␀N_{s}␀␀␀Secondary turns␀Turns on the output winding.␀␀␀␀Vs/Vp = Ns/Np␀␀␀Vp␀␀V_{p}␀␀␀Primary voltage␀What the transformer is fed with.␀␀␀Secondary voltage␀␀␀What comes out, unloaded.␀␀␀E = P*t␀What the meter bills. A kettle boil is about 0.1 kWh.␀␀␀kWh␀The appliance's rating. A fridge averages 100 W.␀␀␀␀How long it runs.␀␀␀E = V*Q␀Stored energy␀␀␀A phone battery holds about 15 Wh, a car battery 600 Wh.␀␀␀␀Wh␀␀Nominal voltage␀Li-ion 3.7 V per cell, lead-acid 12 V.␀␀Capacity␀␀␀␀The rating in Ah or mAh.␀␀␀␀Ah␀␀t = Q/I␀Run time␀␀␀␀From full to empty.␀On the label. A AA is about 2500 mAh, a phone 4000 mAh.␀mAh␀Average draw of the device.␀R = R0*(1 + alpha*dT)␀␀␀Resistance when hot␀At the new temperature.␀R0␀␀R_{0}␀␀␀Resistance at reference␀Usually quoted at 20 C.␀alpha␀␀␀Î±␀␀Temperature coefficient␀Fractional change per kelvin. Copper 0.00393.␀␀␀Copper=0.00393|Aluminium=0.00429|Silver=0.0038|Tungsten=0.0045|Iron=0.00651|Nichrome=0.0004␀Î”T␀Temperature change␀␀Above the reference temperature.␀␀␀␀C deg␀␀␀Vd = 2*I*rho*L/A␀␀␀␀Vd␀␀V_{d}␀␀␀Voltage drop␀␀␀␀Lost in the cable. Keep it under about 3% of the supply.␀␀␀␀Drawn by the load.␀␀Cable length␀␀␀␀One way: the current travels there and back.␀␀␀␀Cross-section␀␀␀Of one conductor. 2.5 mm^2 is a common power cable.␀mm^2␀␀␀␀A property of the metal, measured in ohm-metres. Copper 1.68e-8.␀␀␀␀R = rho*L/A␀A = pi*d^2/4␀␀␀␀R = 4*rho*L/(pi*d^2)␀␀␀␀End to end. 18 AWG copper is about 21 milliohm per metre.␀␀␀Of the wire, one way.␀␀␀Of the metal. 1.5 mm^2 is house lighting wire.␀␀Of the metal, without the insulation.␀␀␀R = (Vs - Vf)/I␀P = I^2*R␀␀␀P = (Vs - Vf)*I␀P = (Vs - Vf)^2/R␀␀␀What feeds the LED and its resistor. 3.3 V, 5 V, 12 V.␀␀Vf␀␀V_{f}␀␀␀LED forward voltage␀Red about 2 V, green 2.2 V, blue and white 3.1 V.␀␀␀Red=2.0|Yellow=2.1|Green=2.2|Blue=3.1|White=3.1|Infrared=1.3␀␀␀␀LED current␀Indicators run at 2 to 20 mA.␀␀␀Pick the next standard value up.␀␀␀␀Resistor power␀␀Heat in the resistor. Choose a rating about twice this.␀I1 = I*R2/(R1 + R2)␀I = I1*(R1 + R2)/R2␀R1 = R2*(I - I1)/I1␀R2 = R1*I1/(I - I1)␀Total current␀␀␀Going into the pair.␀␀␀␀I1␀␀I_{1}␀␀␀Current in R1␀␀␀The smaller resistor takes the larger share.␀␀␀␀Resistor 1␀␀The branch whose current is I1.␀Resistor 2␀␀The other branch.␀␀␀Vout = Vin*R2/(R1 + R2)␀Vin = Vout*(R1 + R2)/R2␀R1 = R2*(Vin - Vout)/Vout␀␀␀R2 = R1*Vout/(Vin - Vout)␀␀␀Across the whole divider. 5 V, 12 V...␀␀Across R2, with nothing loading it.␀Top resistor␀␀␀␀From the input to the output.␀␀␀Bottom resistor␀From the output to ground.␀␀L = L1*L2/(L1 + L2)␀1/L = 1/L1 + 1/L2␀␀␀L = L1 + L2␀Total inductance␀␀␀␀What the pair acts like, with no coupling between them.␀L1␀␀L_{1}␀␀␀Inductor 1␀␀Power inductors run from 1 uH to 1 mH.␀␀L2␀␀L_{2}␀␀␀Inductor 2␀␀The second inductor.␀␀␀␀C = C1*C2/(C1 + C2)␀1/C = 1/C1 + 1/C2␀␀␀C = C1 + C2␀Total capacitance␀␀␀What the pair acts like.␀␀␀␀C1␀␀C_{1}␀␀␀Capacitor 1␀100 nF is the usual decoupling value.␀␀␀C2␀␀C_{2}␀␀␀Capacitor 2␀The second capacitor.␀␀␀R = 1/(1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3)␀␀1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3␀␀␀␀R = R1*R2/(R1 + R2)␀1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2␀␀␀R = R1 + R2 + R3␀␀␀␀Total resistance␀␀␀␀What the chain looks like from outside.␀First in the chain.␀Second in the chain.␀␀␀␀R3␀␀R_{3}␀␀␀Resistor 3␀␀Third in the chain.␀R = R1 + R2␀What the pair looks like from outside.␀␀A 1 kohm resistor is a common value.␀␀␀␀The second resistor in the chain.␀␀␀V = I*R␀P = V*I␀P = V^2/R␀␀␀Across the part. A AA cell is 1.5 V, a car 12 V, US mains 120 V.␀␀␀␀Through the part. An LED takes 20 mA, a kettle about 10 A.␀␀How hard it is for current to flow. Resistors run from ohms to megohms.␀Heat or work per second. A small resistor is rated 0.25 W, a heater 1500 W.␀ideal-gas␀␀␀Ideal gas law␀␀␀pv nrt moles pressure volume temperature␀␀␀␀How pressure, volume, amount and temperature tie together.␀␀combined-gas␀␀␀␀Combined gas law␀␀␀␀charles gay lussac before after␀A fixed amount of gas, before and after a change.␀␀␀boyle␀␀␀Boyle's law␀pressure volume squeeze isothermal␀␀Squeeze a gas at steady temperature and the pressure rises.␀heat␀␀␀␀Heat capacity␀␀␀specific heat energy mcdt warming kettle␀␀␀␀The heat to warm something up.␀␀latent␀␀Latent heat␀melting boiling evaporation fusion vaporisation␀The heat to melt or boil something, at steady temperature.␀␀flow-heat␀␀␀Heat in a flow␀␀water loop radiator cooling hvac␀␀␀␀Heat carried by a stream of fluid.␀␀conduction␀␀Conduction␀␀fourier insulation wall heat loss␀␀␀Heat through a solid wall.␀␀convection␀␀Convection␀␀newton heat transfer air cooling heatsink␀␀␀Heat from a surface to a moving fluid.␀␀Thermal radiation␀␀␀stefan boltzmann infrared blackbody emissivity␀␀Heat radiated from a hot surface to cooler surroundings.␀␀␀␀carnot␀␀Carnot efficiency␀␀␀heat engine limit second law thermodynamics␀The best any heat engine can do between two temperatures.␀␀␀newton-cooling␀␀Newton's cooling␀␀␀␀coffee temperature decay exponential␀␀␀␀Things cool quickly at first, then slowly towards the room.␀T = Ta + (T0 - Ta)*exp(-k*t)␀␀␀␀Temperature now␀At time t.␀␀Surroundings␀␀␀␀Room temperature.␀␀␀T_{0}␀␀␀Start temperature␀␀␀At time 0.␀␀Cooling rate␀␀␀␀Per second. A coffee cup is about 0.0005.␀␀␀min␀eta = 1 - Tc/Th␀Best efficiency␀Of any heat engine between those temperatures.␀␀Th␀␀T_{h}␀␀␀Hot temperature␀Of the source. Must be absolute; enter in C or F freely.␀␀␀␀Tc␀␀T_